2026-10-07|Remove Invalid Parentheses

Question
Given a string `s` containing parentheses and letters, remove the minimum number of invalid parentheses so that the resulting string is valid.
Return all unique valid strings that can be obtained using the minimum number of removals.

Approach
1. First determine how many extra `(` and `)` must be removed.

2. Scan the string:
   - For `(`, increase the opening balance.
   - For `)`, if an opening parenthesis is available, match it.
   - Otherwise, this `)` is extra and must eventually be removed.

3. Let:
   - `leftRemove` = number of extra `(` to remove.
   - `rightRemove` = number of extra `)` to remove.

4. Use DFS/backtracking to decide for every parenthesis whether to keep it or remove it.

5. Keep track of the current balance:
   - Adding `(` increases the balance.
   - Adding `)` decreases the balance.
   - The balance can never become negative because that would make the prefix invalid.

6. When encountering an invalid `)` and `rightRemove > 0`, try removing it.

7. To avoid duplicate answers, when several consecutive identical parentheses occur, do not remove the same-position equivalent repeatedly.

8. Once the entire string has been processed, a result is valid only when:
   - `leftRemove == 0`
   - `rightRemove == 0`
   - `balance == 0`

9. Insert every valid result into a set to guarantee uniqueness.

10. Time complexity is exponential in the worst case because this is a backtracking problem. The pruning significantly reduces unnecessary states.

11. Space complexity is O(n) for the recursion and the current result, excluding the output.

Solution
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    unordered_set<string> result;

    void dfs(string& s, int index, int balance,
             int leftRemove, int rightRemove,
             string& current) {

        if (balance < 0) {
            return;
        }

        if (leftRemove < 0 || rightRemove < 0) {
            return;
        }

        if (index == s.size()) {
            if (balance == 0 && leftRemove == 0 && rightRemove == 0) {
                result.insert(current);
            }
            return;
        }

        char ch = s[index];

        if (ch == '(') {
            // Remove this '('
            if (leftRemove > 0) {
                dfs(s, index + 1, balance,
                    leftRemove - 1, rightRemove, current);
            }

            // Keep this '('
            current.push_back('(');

            dfs(s, index + 1, balance + 1,
                leftRemove, rightRemove, current);

            current.pop_back();
        }
        else if (ch == ')') {
            // Remove this ')'
            if (rightRemove > 0) {
                dfs(s, index + 1, balance,
                    leftRemove, rightRemove - 1, current);
            }

            // Keep this ')' only if it has a matching '('
            if (balance > 0) {
                current.push_back(')');

                dfs(s, index + 1, balance - 1,
                    leftRemove, rightRemove, current);

                current.pop_back();
            }
        }
        else {
            // Letters are always kept
            current.push_back(ch);

            dfs(s, index + 1, balance,
                leftRemove, rightRemove, current);

            current.pop_back();
        }
    }

    vector<string> removeInvalidParentheses(string s) {
        int leftRemove = 0;
        int rightRemove = 0;

        // Find the minimum number of '(' and ')' to remove.
        for (char ch : s) {
            if (ch == '(') {
                leftRemove++;
            }
            else if (ch == ')') {
                if (leftRemove > 0) {
                    leftRemove--;
                }
                else {
                    rightRemove++;
                }
            }
        }

        result.clear();

        string current;

        dfs(s, 0, 0, leftRemove, rightRemove, current);

        return vector<string>(result.begin(), result.end());
    }
};

int main() {
    string s;
    cin >> s;

    Solution obj;

    vector<string> ans = obj.removeInvalidParentheses(s);

    for (const string& str : ans) {
        cout << str << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
